// std.rt is the runtime (toolchain.md 5).
// D13.1, D13.2
//
// The module does five jobs. It starts and ends the process. It allocates and
// releases heap storage. It writes the runtime error line and the panic line.
// It formats each value the print family writes. It buffers those bytes for
// each descriptor.
// D11.6, D10.2, D10.3, D11.4, D11.7, D12.2, D11.5
//
// The module defines every entry point of toolchain.md 5.1, the two float
// printers included. The compiler holds the list of entry-point names and
// emits calls to them. Everything else below is an ordinary module's own
// code, and a program must not call it (stdlib.md 1.1).
// D18.1, D18.4, D12.2
//
// The two float printers stood in a module of their own until the fold. They
// stood apart because the C bootstrap refuses a float and compiled this file
// into every program it built. It compiles pin 0's library alone now, so this
// file may hold a float and the two modules are one (notes/compiler.md 8).
// D18.1
//
// The module imports std.libc and std.strbuf and no other module. It writes
// every byte through libc.write, and it uses no other part of stdio than the
// snprintf of the float search. So a program prints the bytes the runtime
// error contract and the print family state, not the bytes a C library
// chooses. std.strbuf is there for append_f64 and append_f32, which give a
// buffer the bytes the two printers give a descriptor (stdlib.md 2.12).
// D13.1, D11.4, D11.7, D18.1
//
// Read this paragraph before you edit a failure path. A bounds check or an
// overflow check inside a failure path calls that same failure path. fort has
// no per-function safety switch, so the second failure calls the first one
// again until the stack runs out. The process then writes nothing to stderr.
// This module has no defence against that. What keeps it out of reach is
// arithmetic, and the arithmetic has no slack. Each failure path writes
// through one of three fixed arrays. DECIMAL_MAX is 20 and 20 digits is
// exactly the longest u64. HEX_MAX is 18 and 18 is exactly 2 plus the 16
// digits of the longest pointer. BUFFER_SIZE is 8192 and buffer_append
// flushes the buffer before it copies, or writes the bytes straight to the
// descriptor when they are larger than the whole buffer.
//
// format_u64 and format_hex do not stop at the end of their array. Each one
// runs until the value reaches 0, and each one writes one byte for each digit
// from the end of the array backwards. They stay inside the array because the
// counts above match exactly: the slack is 0 bytes in both. format_i64 takes
// one byte more for a minus sign, and it has that byte because the magnitude
// of a negative i64 is 19 digits at most.
//
// So a new formatter over one of those arrays, or a wider value through an
// old one, underflows start -= 1. That underflow is an overflow check, and
// the check calls fail_overflow, which calls fail_begin, which calls
// format_u64 again. The process then dies with no message at all. Widen the
// array in the same edit that widens the value, and count the digits.
//
// A check that fires in this module is a bug in this module, and it prints
// nothing when it fires. Read the arithmetic above it, not the stack.
import std.libc;
import std.strbuf;

// ---- descriptors and buffers (toolchain.md 5.3) -----------------------------
// D11.5

/// The buffering rule names these two descriptors by number. The runtime
/// buffers stdout. It does not buffer stderr. It tells the two apart by the
/// number alone.
/// D11.5
i32 STDOUT_FD = 1;
i32 STDERR_FD = 2;

/// How many bytes one descriptor holds back before a write. toolchain.md 5.3
/// leaves this size to the runtime.
u64 BUFFER_SIZE = 8192;

/// How many descriptors the runtime buffers at once: stdout plus this many
/// others. A program that writes to more descriptors than this takes the
/// oldest buffer back. buffer_for flushes that buffer and gives it to the new
/// descriptor.
u64 BUFFER_POOL_SIZE = 8;

/// Slot 0 always holds stdout. The pool follows it.
u64 BUFFER_COUNT = 1 + BUFFER_POOL_SIZE;

/// exit keeps one byte of the status.
/// D11.6
i32 EXIT_STATUS_MASK = 0xFF;

u64 DECIMAL_BASE = 10;
u64 HEX_BASE = 16;

/// The longest decimal text: -9223372036854775808, or 18446744073709551615.
u64 DECIMAL_MAX = 20;

/// The longest pointer text: 0x and 16 hex digits.
u64 HEX_MAX = 18;

/// The largest u64. alloc divides it to test a product for overflow.
u64 U64_MAX = 18446744073709551615;

/// The print family fixes the case of a printed pointer, so these digits are
/// lowercase.
/// D11.7
string HEX_DIGITS = "0123456789abcdef";

/// The bytes one descriptor still holds. fd is -1 while the slot is free.
/// is_line_buffered holds the answer isatty gave when buffer_for took the
/// slot. len counts the pending bytes in data. len is never larger than
/// BUFFER_SIZE.
/// D11.5
struct buffer {
    i32 fd;
    bool is_line_buffered;
    u64 len;
    u8[BUFFER_SIZE] data;
}

/// One row of the table print_enum reads. This is the fort type of the
/// %fort.enum_member the compiler emits (toolchain.md 6 items 2 and 21). The
/// two fields are that type's two fields, in that order.
/// D3.9, D12.2
struct enum_member {
    i32 value;
    char* name;
}

/// Slot 0 holds stdout. buffer_for gives the other slots out in order. When
/// every slot is taken, it takes them back in the same order.
///
/// These nine buffers and the message buffer below hold 82080 bytes together.
/// Every closure holds this module, so every program carries those bytes in
/// BSS.
/// D9.10
buffer[BUFFER_COUNT] mut buffers = {};
bool mut buffers_ready = false;
u64 mut next_eviction = 1;

/// A failure path builds its line in this buffer. The line then reaches stderr
/// in one write, if it fits.
/// D11.4
buffer mut message = {};

/// The string@ args_init builds from argv at process start. args hands it out
/// as a view. It lives as long as the process does.
/// D11.6
string mut@ own mut args_store = {};

/// Answers whether fd is a terminal. A terminal makes the buffer
/// line-buffered. The runtime asks this once for each buffer, when it takes
/// the slot. It never asks it for a write, so no print costs a system call of
/// its own.
/// D11.5
///
/// isatty fails with ENOTTY on every descriptor that is not a terminal, and it
/// writes that errno. A program reads errno through sys.errno() after a call
/// of its own, so this function saves errno and writes it back
/// (toolchain.md 5.3). Without the restore, the first print after a failed
/// open reports ENOTTY instead of the program's own error.
fn is_interactive(i32 fd) bool {
    i32 mut* slot = libc.errno_slot();
    i32 saved = *slot;
    bool interactive = libc.isatty(fd) == 1;
    *slot = saved;
    return interactive;
}

fn buffers_init() void {
    if (buffers_ready) {
        return;
    }
    buffers[0].fd = STDOUT_FD;
    buffers[0].is_line_buffered = is_interactive(STDOUT_FD);
    for (u64 mut i = 1; i < BUFFER_COUNT; i++) {
        buffers[i].fd = -1;
        buffers[i].is_line_buffered = false;
    }
    // The failure line is assembled whole and written by its own flush, so it
    // is never cut at a newline.
    // D11.4
    message.fd = STDERR_FD;
    message.is_line_buffered = false;
    buffers_ready = true;
}

/// Writes every byte of bytes to fd. It asks again after a short write and
/// after EINTR. On any other error it drops the bytes it has not written
/// (toolchain.md 5.3).
///
/// No test covers either retry, and no test here can. POSIX makes a blocking
/// write to a pipe or to a file return only after it has written every byte,
/// and std.libc declares no pipe, no mkfifo and no socket, so no fort program
/// can make a short write. O_NONBLOCK does nothing on a regular file. The
/// EINTR branch needs a signal to arrive during the write, which no fort
/// program can arrange either. Both branches stay because both cases are real
/// outside this harness.
fn write_all(i32 fd, u8@ bytes) void {
    u64 mut done = 0;
    while (done < bytes.len) {
        u8@ rest = bytes[done..];
        i64 n = libc.write(fd, rest.ptr, rest.len);
        if (n < 0) {
            if (*libc.errno_slot() == libc.EINTR) {
                continue;
            }
            return;
        }
        done += cast(n, u64);
    }
}

fn buffer_flush(buffer mut* b) void {
    if (b->len == 0) {
        return;
    }
    // This reads len and clears it before the write. A write that fails then
    // drops those bytes. It does not offer them again (toolchain.md 5.3).
    u64 len = b->len;
    b->len = 0;
    write_all(b->fd, b->data[..len]);
}

/// Answers whether the bytes hold a newline. A newline ends a line for a
/// line-buffered descriptor.
/// D11.5
fn has_newline(u8@ bytes) bool {
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < bytes.len; i++) {
        if (bytes[i] == cast('\n', u8)) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

/// Adds the bytes to the buffer. It flushes first when they do not fit. It
/// writes them straight to the descriptor when they are larger than the whole
/// buffer. After a write to a line-buffered descriptor that holds a newline,
/// it flushes the whole buffer, which is what C does. Bytes that went straight
/// to the descriptor are already out.
/// D11.5
fn buffer_append(buffer mut* b, u8@ bytes) void {
    u64 n = bytes.len;
    if (n == 0) {
        return;
    }
    if (n > BUFFER_SIZE - b->len) {
        buffer_flush(b);
    }
    if (n > BUFFER_SIZE) {
        write_all(b->fd, bytes);
        return;
    }
    // The flush above leaves room for n bytes. The span and the copy below
    // therefore stay inside data.
    u8 mut@ room = b->data[b->len..];
    libc.memcpy(room.ptr, bytes.ptr, n);
    b->len += n;
    if (b->is_line_buffered && has_newline(bytes)) {
        buffer_flush(b);
    }
}

/// Answers with the buffer of fd, and takes a slot for fd when it has none. It
/// answers null for stderr, which the runtime does not buffer, and for a
/// negative fd, whose writes fail and whose bytes the runtime drops.
fn buffer_for(i32 fd) buffer mut* {
    if (fd == STDERR_FD || fd < 0) {
        return null;
    }
    buffers_init();
    u64 mut free_slot = 0;
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < BUFFER_COUNT; i++) {
        if (buffers[i].fd == fd) {
            return &buffers[i];
        }
        if (buffers[i].fd < 0 && free_slot == 0) {
            free_slot = i;
        }
    }
    // Slot 0 holds stdout, so free_slot 0 means the loop found no free slot.
    // This takes the oldest slot back.
    if (free_slot == 0) {
        free_slot = next_eviction;
        next_eviction = 1 + (next_eviction % BUFFER_POOL_SIZE);
        buffer_flush(&buffers[free_slot]);
    }
    buffers[free_slot].fd = fd;
    buffers[free_slot].is_line_buffered = is_interactive(fd);
    buffers[free_slot].len = 0;
    return &buffers[free_slot];
}

/// Sends the bytes to fd through its buffer. For stderr it writes them
/// straight to the descriptor.
fn emit(i32 fd, u8@ bytes) void {
    buffer mut* b = buffer_for(fd);
    if (b == null) {
        write_all(fd, bytes);
        return;
    }
    buffer_append(b, bytes);
}

/// Writes the bytes of a fort string. This module writes the fixed texts of
/// the print family and of the runtime error contract this way.
/// D11.7, D11.4
fn emit_str(i32 fd, string s) void {
    emit(fd, cast(s, u8@));
}

/// Writes the bytes of a NUL-terminated C string. An enum member name is such
/// a string, and so is every file name the compiler passes
/// (toolchain.md 5.1).
fn emit_cstr(i32 fd, char* s) void {
    emit(fd, cast(s[0..libc.strlen(s)], u8@));
}

/// Writes out the buffer of fd. It does nothing when fd has no buffer.
/// io.flush and io.close call it (stdlib.md 3).
/// D11.5
fn flush(i32 fd) void {
    buffers_init();
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < BUFFER_COUNT; i++) {
        if (buffers[i].fd == fd) {
            buffer_flush(&buffers[i]);
            return;
        }
    }
}

/// Writes out every buffer. exit calls it, every failure path calls it, and a
/// program calls it before it writes through libc.write (stdlib.md 3).
/// D11.5
fn flush_all() void {
    buffers_init();
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < BUFFER_COUNT; i++) {
        if (buffers[i].fd >= 0) {
            buffer_flush(&buffers[i]);
        }
    }
}

// ---- formatting -------------------------------------------------------------
// D11.7

/// Writes the decimal digits of v at the end of out. It answers with the index
/// of the first digit. out holds DECIMAL_MAX bytes, which is the longest u64.
/// The loop writes one digit before it tests v, so 0 prints as one digit.
fn format_u64(u8 mut@ out, u64 v) u64 {
    u64 mut start = out.len;
    u64 mut rest = v;
    while (true) {
        start -= 1;
        out[start] = cast('0', u8) + cast(rest % DECIMAL_BASE, u8);
        rest /= DECIMAL_BASE;
        if (rest == 0) {
            return start;
        }
    }
}

/// Does what format_u64 does, and writes a minus before the digits of a
/// negative v. No i64 holds the magnitude of i64's minimum, so the subtraction
/// below runs in u64 and wraps. A subtraction that must wrap is spelled -%.
/// That magnitude is 19 digits, so out keeps one byte for the minus.
/// D11.2
fn format_i64(u8 mut@ out, i64 v) u64 {
    if (v >= 0) {
        return format_u64(out, cast(v, u64));
    }
    u64 magnitude = 0 -% cast(v, u64);
    u64 start = format_u64(out, magnitude) - 1;
    out[start] = cast('-', u8);
    return start;
}

/// Writes 0x and the lowercase hex digits of v at the end of out, with no
/// leading zero. It writes 0x0 for 0. out holds HEX_MAX bytes. It answers with
/// the index of the first byte of the text.
/// D11.7
fn format_hex(u8 mut@ out, u64 v) u64 {
    u64 mut start = out.len;
    u64 mut rest = v;
    while (true) {
        start -= 1;
        out[start] = cast(HEX_DIGITS[rest % HEX_BASE], u8);
        rest /= HEX_BASE;
        if (rest == 0) {
            start -= 1;
            out[start] = cast('x', u8);
            start -= 1;
            out[start] = cast('0', u8);
            return start;
        }
    }
}

fn emit_u64(i32 fd, u64 v) void {
    u8[DECIMAL_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 start = format_u64(out[..], v);
    emit(fd, out[start..]);
}

fn emit_i64(i32 fd, i64 v) void {
    u8[DECIMAL_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 start = format_i64(out[..], v);
    emit(fd, out[start..]);
}

fn print_i64(i32 fd, i64 v) void {
    emit_i64(fd, v);
}

fn print_u64(i32 fd, u64 v) void {
    emit_u64(fd, v);
}

/// Writes true or false. The print family fixes those two texts.
///
/// The conditional expression is deliberate. The C bootstrap refuses this
/// form, and this file is in the import closure of every program, so the
/// build works only because a pinned fort compiler builds the library now.
/// It is the smallest such form the library can spell without a float, and
/// notes/compiler.md 8 asks the next reader to leave it here.
/// D7.5, D11.7
fn print_bool(i32 fd, bool v) void {
    emit_str(fd, v ? "true" : "false");
}

/// Writes the byte of c. The print family writes a char as its byte, whatever
/// that byte is.
/// D11.7
fn print_char(i32 fd, char c) void {
    u8[1] mut one = {};
    one[0] = cast(c, u8);
    emit(fd, one[..]);
}

/// Writes 0x and lowercase hex digits. It writes 0x0 for null.
/// D11.7
fn print_ptr(i32 fd, void* p) void {
    u8[HEX_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 start = format_hex(out[..], cast(p, u64));
    emit(fd, out[start..]);
}

/// Writes the bytes of a string. len is the string's own length. It counts no
/// terminator. The zero string has no byte to write.
/// D11.7
fn print_str(i32 fd, char* ptr, u64 len) void {
    if (len == 0) {
        return;
    }
    emit(fd, cast(ptr[0..len], u8@));
}

/// Writes the name of the first member whose value is v. It writes v itself
/// when no member holds v. m points at the table the compiler emits and n
/// counts its rows (toolchain.md 6 item 21).
/// D11.7, D7.7
fn print_enum(i32 fd, i32 v, enum_member* m, u64 n) void {
    enum_member@ table = m[0..n];
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (table[i].value == v) {
            emit_cstr(fd, table[i].name);
            return;
        }
    }
    emit_i64(fd, cast(v, i64));
}

// ---- the float printers (toolchain.md 5.1) ----------------------------------
// D18.1, D18.2, D18.3
//
// What they produce. The digits are the shortest decimal that reads back as
// the value in the value's own type and, among the texts of that length that
// read back, the one nearest the value, ties to the even last digit. The
// layout around them: exponent form when the decimal exponent of the leading
// digit is below -4 or at 17 and above, fixed form between, `.0` appended
// when a fixed text has neither a point nor an exponent, the exponent written
// as `e`, a sign and at least two digits, `-0.0` for a negative zero, and
// `nan` for every NaN whatever its sign bit, since the print family lists
// `inf`, `-inf` and `nan` and no `-nan`.
// D18.2, D18.3, D11.7
//
// How they produce them, which the shortest-digit rule leaves open and
// toolchain.md 5.1 describes: snprintf is asked for one significant digit,
// then two, and so on, and the first length whose text strtod reads back as
// the value wins. 17 digits for an f64 and 9 for an f32 always read back, so
// the search ends. snprintf returns the nearest decimal of the length asked
// for, which is not always the one to keep: for a normal power of two above
// the least normal the values that read back reach half an ulp above the value
// and only a quarter below, the binade below being coarser, so the nearest
// decimal can fall short of that interval while the next one up falls inside
// it. That neighbour is tried before the length grows. The neighbour below
// never needs trying, since the gap below a float is never wider than the gap
// above.
// D18.2
//
// This is the one place in the runtime that asks the C library for a
// formatted value. It asks two things of it which the C standard permits but
// does not require and glibc provides: a correctly rounded printf, ties to
// even, and a correctly rounded strtod down to the subnormals. strtod reports
// ERANGE there, which is ignored, since only the value it returns matters.
//
// Read this paragraph before you change a buffer or a loop bound, for the
// reason the head of this file states at its own. The bounds here are exact
// and the arithmetic has no slack. A decimal holds 17 digits, which is every
// digit an f64 needs and eight more than an f32 needs. TEXT_MAX is 40 and the
// longest text is 24 bytes: a sign, a digit, a point, sixteen digits and
// `e-308`. SCRATCH_MAX is 32 and the longest text snprintf writes into it is
// `%.16e` of a finite double, which is 23 bytes and a NUL. EXPONENT_MAX is 8
// and an exponent is at most three digits, since 308 and 324 are the widest
// an f64 reaches.

/// The significant digits an f64 and an f32 need at most: 17 and 9 always read
/// back, which is what ends the search.
/// D18.2
u64 DIGITS_F64 = 17;
u64 DIGITS_F32 = 9;

/// The decimal exponent of the leading digit at which the form changes: below
/// the first is exponent form, at the second and above is exponent form,
/// between them is fixed form.
/// D18.3
i32 EXP_LOW = -4;
i32 EXP_HIGH = 17;

/// The least number of exponent digits the print family writes, so 1e5 prints
/// `1e+05`.
/// D11.7
u64 EXP_MIN_DIGITS = 2;

/// The bytes each buffer holds; the paragraph above states why each is enough.
u64 TEXT_MAX = 40;
u64 TEXT_MAX_NUL = 41;
u64 SCRATCH_MAX = 32;
u64 EXPONENT_MAX = 8;

/// The largest finite value of each type, which is what tells a finite value
/// from an infinity: a comparison is false for a NaN either way, so the NaN is
/// taken first.
/// D6.12
f64 F64_MAX = 1.7976931348623157e308;
f32 F32_MAX = 3.4028234663852886e38;

/// The bit that carries the sign of a zero, since -0.0 == 0.0 and no comparison
/// can tell the two apart.
/// D6.12, D18.3
u64 F64_SIGN_SHIFT = 63;
u32 F32_SIGN_SHIFT = 31;

/// The bytes of an f64 and of an f32, which the sign test copies.
u64 F64_BYTES = 8;
u64 F32_BYTES = 4;

/// The format snprintf is asked for: a decimal with `precision` digits after
/// the point, in exponent form. `%.*e` takes the precision as an argument, so
/// one text serves every length.
string FORMAT = "%.*e";

/// The C library the search needs. std.libc wraps snprintf for both target ABIs.
/// The two parsers stand here because the search is their one caller.
/// D9.10, D9.8, D13.2
extern fn strtod(char* s, void* end) f64;
extern fn strtof(char* s, void* end) f32;

/// One float as a decimal: the significant digits of the shortest text that
/// reads back as the value, most significant first, and the power of ten the
/// first of them stands for, so the value is digit[0].digit[1..] times ten to
/// the exponent. The sign is kept apart, since -0.0 has no digit to carry it.
struct decimal {
    u64 digit_count;
    i32 exponent;
    u8[DIGITS_F64] digit;
}

// ---- reading snprintf's text ------------------------------------------------

fn is_digit(char c) bool {
    return c >= '0' && c <= '9';
}

fn digit_of(char c) u64 {
    return cast(c, u64) - cast('0', u64);
}

fn char_of(u64 digit) char {
    return cast(cast('0', u64) + digit, char);
}

/// Reads printf's %e text back into digits and an exponent. The text is
/// d.ddde+xx, without the point at precision 0, and never carries a sign here:
/// only the magnitude is printed. A text with no digit at all, which is what a
/// failed printf leaves behind, scans as the decimal zero, so every caller gets
/// a decimal it can lay out.
fn scan(char* s, decimal mut* d) void {
    char@ text = s[0..libc.strlen(s)];
    d->digit_count = 0;
    d->exponent = 0;
    u64 mut i = 0;
    while (i < text.len && text[i] != 'e') {
        if (is_digit(text[i]) && d->digit_count < DIGITS_F64) {
            d->digit[d->digit_count] = cast(digit_of(text[i]), u8);
            d->digit_count += 1;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    if (d->digit_count == 0) {
        d->digit[0] = 0;
        d->digit_count = 1;
    }
    if (i >= text.len) {
        return;
    }
    // The `e` and what follows it.
    i += 1;
    bool mut negative = false;
    if (i < text.len && (text[i] == '+' || text[i] == '-')) {
        negative = text[i] == '-';
        i += 1;
    }
    i32 mut exponent = 0;
    while (i < text.len && is_digit(text[i])) {
        exponent = exponent * cast(DECIMAL_BASE, i32) + cast(digit_of(text[i]), i32);
        i += 1;
    }
    if (negative) {
        d->exponent = -exponent;
    } else {
        d->exponent = exponent;
    }
}

/// The next decimal of the same length upward: 1.24 after 1.23, and a power of
/// ten after all nines, where the digits collapse to a single one. That last
/// case is here for totality and no value can be printed by it: digits that are
/// all nines mean the value is within a hundredth of the power of ten above, so
/// that power of ten was the nearest one-digit decimal and was tried, and
/// rejected, at the very first length.
fn step_up(decimal mut* d) void {
    u64 mut i = d->digit_count;
    while (i > 0) {
        i -= 1;
        if (cast(d->digit[i], u64) < DECIMAL_BASE - 1) {
            d->digit[i] += 1;
            return;
        }
        d->digit[i] = 0;
    }
    d->digit[0] = 1;
    d->digit_count = 1;
    d->exponent += 1;
}

// ---- the float layout -------------------------------------------------------
// D18.3

/// Appends the exponent as `e`, a sign and at least two digits. The magnitude
/// is at most three digits, so the buffer of EXPONENT_MAX bytes it builds them
/// in has five to spare.
/// D11.7
fn put_exponent(char mut@ out, u64 at, i32 exponent) u64 {
    u64 mut n = at;
    out[n] = 'e';
    n += 1;
    u64 mut magnitude = cast(exponent, u64);
    if (exponent < 0) {
        out[n] = '-';
        magnitude = cast(-exponent, u64);
    } else {
        out[n] = '+';
    }
    n += 1;
    char[EXPONENT_MAX] mut text = {};
    u64 mut start = EXPONENT_MAX;
    u64 mut rest = magnitude;
    while (true) {
        start -= 1;
        text[start] = char_of(rest % DECIMAL_BASE);
        rest /= DECIMAL_BASE;
        if (rest == 0) {
            break;
        }
    }
    while (EXPONENT_MAX - start < EXP_MIN_DIGITS) {
        start -= 1;
        text[start] = '0';
    }
    while (start < EXPONENT_MAX) {
        out[n] = text[start];
        n += 1;
        start += 1;
    }
    return n;
}

/// Lays the digits out: exponent form below 1e-4 and at 1e17 and above, fixed
/// form between, with `.0` appended when the fixed text has neither a point nor
/// an exponent. `out` holds TEXT_MAX bytes; the result is how many were
/// written.
/// D18.3
fn layout(bool negative, decimal* d, char mut@ out) u64 {
    u64 mut n = 0;
    if (negative) {
        out[n] = '-';
        n += 1;
    }
    if (d->exponent < EXP_LOW || d->exponent >= EXP_HIGH) {
        out[n] = char_of(cast(d->digit[0], u64));
        n += 1;
        if (d->digit_count > 1) {
            out[n] = '.';
            n += 1;
            for (u64 mut i = 1; i < d->digit_count; i += 1) {
                out[n] = char_of(cast(d->digit[i], u64));
                n += 1;
            }
        }
        return put_exponent(out, n, d->exponent);
    }
    if (d->exponent < 0) {
        out[n] = '0';
        n += 1;
        out[n] = '.';
        n += 1;
        // The zeros between the point and the first digit.
        for (i32 mut i = -1; i > d->exponent; i -= 1) {
            out[n] = '0';
            n += 1;
        }
        for (u64 mut i = 0; i < d->digit_count; i += 1) {
            out[n] = char_of(cast(d->digit[i], u64));
            n += 1;
        }
        return n;
    }
    u64 integer_digits = cast(d->exponent, u64) + 1;
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < integer_digits; i += 1) {
        // The integer part is padded with zeros when the digits run out
        // before the point.
        if (i < d->digit_count) {
            out[n] = char_of(cast(d->digit[i], u64));
        } else {
            out[n] = '0';
        }
        n += 1;
    }
    out[n] = '.';
    n += 1;
    if (d->digit_count <= integer_digits) {
        out[n] = '0';
        n += 1;
        return n;
    }
    for (u64 mut i = integer_digits; i < d->digit_count; i += 1) {
        out[n] = char_of(cast(d->digit[i], u64));
        n += 1;
    }
    return n;
}

// ---- the shortest-digit search ----------------------------------------------
// D18.2

/// The decimal as a NUL-terminated text, laid out as it would print, which is
/// the text the reader is given.
fn decimal_text(decimal* d, char mut@ buf) void {
    u64 n = layout(false, d, buf);
    buf[n] = '\0';
}

/// Whether the decimal reads back as `v`, in each type. strtod and strtof are
/// correctly rounded down to the subnormals, where they also report ERANGE;
/// only the value matters here, so errno is left alone.
/// D18.2
fn reads_back_f64(decimal* d, f64 v) bool {
    char[TEXT_MAX_NUL] mut text = {};
    decimal_text(d, text[..]);
    return strtod(&text[0], null) == v;
}

fn reads_back_f32(decimal* d, f32 v) bool {
    char[TEXT_MAX_NUL] mut text = {};
    decimal_text(d, text[..]);
    return strtof(&text[0], null) == v;
}

/// The shortest decimal that reads back as `v` in f64, and of the texts of that
/// length the one nearest `v`; the module's header describes the search and why
/// the neighbour above is tried. `v` is a finite magnitude, never negative and
/// never a NaN.
/// D18.2
fn decimal_f64(f64 v, decimal mut* d) void {
    char[SCRATCH_MAX] mut scratch = {};
    for (u64 mut precision = 0; precision < DIGITS_F64; precision += 1) {
        scratch[0] = '\0';
        if (libc.snprintf_float(&scratch[0], SCRATCH_MAX, FORMAT.ptr,
                                cast(precision, i32), v) < 0) {
            // A printf that fails leaves the text empty, which scans as zero.
            scratch[0] = '\0';
        }
        scan(&scratch[0], d);
        if (precision == DIGITS_F64 - 1 || reads_back_f64(d, v)) {
            return;
        }
        decimal mut up = *d;
        step_up(&up);
        if (reads_back_f64(&up, v)) {
            *d = up;
            return;
        }
    }
}

/// The same search in f32, where 9 digits always read back and strtof decides.
/// The magnitude widens to f64 for snprintf without changing value.
/// D18.2: the shortest decimal that reads back in its own type
fn decimal_f32(f32 v, decimal mut* d) void {
    char[SCRATCH_MAX] mut scratch = {};
    for (u64 mut precision = 0; precision < DIGITS_F32; precision += 1) {
        scratch[0] = '\0';
        if (libc.snprintf_float(&scratch[0], SCRATCH_MAX, FORMAT.ptr,
                                cast(precision, i32), cast(v, f64)) < 0) {
            scratch[0] = '\0';
        }
        scan(&scratch[0], d);
        if (precision == DIGITS_F32 - 1 || reads_back_f32(d, v)) {
            return;
        }
        decimal mut up = *d;
        step_up(&up);
        if (reads_back_f32(&up, v)) {
            *d = up;
            return;
        }
    }
}

// ---- the two float entry points ---------------------------------------------
// D18.1

/// The sign bit of the value: -0.0 == 0.0, so a comparison cannot tell the two
/// apart and the bytes decide. std.math holds the same bit cast, as f64_bits
/// (stdlib.md 2.9), and the runtime does not import it: this module is in
/// every closure, so every program pays for what it imports. The cast is a
/// memcpy here, which is what stdlib.md's own sketch of f64_bits does.
/// D6.12, D9.10
fn negative_f64(f64 v) bool {
    u64 mut bits = 0;
    f64 mut source = v;
    libc.memcpy(cast(&bits, u8 mut*), cast(&source, u8*), F64_BYTES);
    return (bits >> F64_SIGN_SHIFT) != 0;
}

fn negative_f32(f32 v) bool {
    u32 mut bits = 0;
    f32 mut source = v;
    libc.memcpy(cast(&bits, u8 mut*), cast(&source, u8*), F32_BYTES);
    return (bits >> F32_SIGN_SHIFT) != 0;
}

/// The text of a value in `out`, and how many bytes it is: everything
/// print_f64 does but the writing. It stands apart from the entry point so
/// that the bytes can be held against a table without a descriptor, which is
/// what run/stdlib/094 does; `out` holds TEXT_MAX bytes.
fn text_f64(f64 v, char mut@ out) u64 {
    if (v != v) {
        // The print family lists inf, -inf and nan and no -nan, so a NaN
        // prints the same whatever its sign bit.
        // D11.7
        return fixed_text(out, "nan");
    }
    bool negative = negative_f64(v);
    if (v > F64_MAX || v < -F64_MAX) {
        // Every finite f64 is at most F64_MAX in magnitude, so a larger one is
        // an infinity.
        if (negative) {
            return fixed_text(out, "-inf");
        }
        return fixed_text(out, "inf");
    }
    f64 mut magnitude = v;
    if (negative) {
        magnitude = -v;
    }
    decimal mut d = decimal{};
    decimal_f64(magnitude, &d);
    return layout(negative, &d, out);
}

/// The same for an f32. An f32 is never widened to an f64 first, because the
/// digits printed depend on the argument's own type.
/// D18.1
fn text_f32(f32 v, char mut@ out) u64 {
    if (v != v) {
        return fixed_text(out, "nan");
    }
    bool negative = negative_f32(v);
    if (v > F32_MAX || v < -F32_MAX) {
        if (negative) {
            return fixed_text(out, "-inf");
        }
        return fixed_text(out, "inf");
    }
    f32 mut magnitude = v;
    if (negative) {
        magnitude = -v;
    }
    decimal mut d = decimal{};
    decimal_f32(magnitude, &d);
    return layout(negative, &d, out);
}

/// One of the three texts the print family fixes, copied into the caller's
/// buffer.
/// D11.7
fn fixed_text(char mut@ out, string text) u64 {
    u64 mut i = 0;
    while (i < text.len) {
        out[i] = text[i];
        i += 1;
    }
    return text.len;
}

/// Writes the text of an f64 to the buffer of `fd`.
/// D11.7, D18.1
fn print_f64(i32 fd, f64 v) void {
    char[TEXT_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 n = text_f64(v, out[..]);
    emit(fd, cast(out[0..n], u8@));
}

/// The same for an f32.
fn print_f32(i32 fd, f32 v) void {
    char[TEXT_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 n = text_f32(v, out[..]);
    emit(fd, cast(out[0..n], u8@));
}

/// Appends the text of an f64 to `b` (stdlib.md 2.12). It is print_f64 with a
/// buffer in place of the descriptor: both take their bytes from text_f64, so
/// the two forms cannot disagree over a value. This is why the module imports
/// std.strbuf. The buffer form stands here and not in std.strbuf so that one
/// text_f64 serves both, which is what keeps them from disagreeing.
/// D18.1
fn append_f64(strbuf.str_buf mut* b, f64 v) void {
    char[TEXT_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 n = text_f64(v, out[..]);
    strbuf.append_bytes(b, cast(out[0..n], u8@));
}

/// The same for an f32, whose digits are its own type's.
/// D18.1
fn append_f32(strbuf.str_buf mut* b, f32 v) void {
    char[TEXT_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 n = text_f32(v, out[..]);
    strbuf.append_bytes(b, cast(out[0..n], u8@));
}

// ---- failures (toolchain.md 5.2) --------------------------------------------
// D11.4

fn message_str(string s) void {
    buffer_append(&message, cast(s, u8@));
}

fn message_cstr(char* s) void {
    buffer_append(&message, cast(s[0..libc.strlen(s)], u8@));
}

fn message_u64(u64 v) void {
    u8[DECIMAL_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 start = format_u64(out[..], v);
    buffer_append(&message, out[start..]);
}

fn message_i64(i64 v) void {
    u8[DECIMAL_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 start = format_i64(out[..], v);
    buffer_append(&message, out[start..]);
}

/// Flushes every buffer. It then starts the failure line with the position and
/// the kind. The kind is "runtime error", "panic" or "assertion failed". The
/// runtime error contract fixes this shape for all three.
/// D11.4
fn fail_begin(char* file, u32 line, u32 col, string kind) void {
    flush_all();
    message.len = 0;
    message_cstr(file);
    message_str(":");
    message_u64(cast(line, u64));
    message_str(":");
    message_u64(cast(col, u64));
    message_str(": ");
    message_str(kind);
    message_str(": ");
}

/// Ends the line, writes it, and calls abort. The process then ends with
/// SIGABRT.
/// D11.4
fn fail_end() noreturn {
    message_str("\n");
    buffer_flush(&message);
    libc.abort();
}

fn fail_runtime(char* file, u32 line, u32 col, string what) noreturn {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "runtime error");
    message_str(what);
    fail_end();
}

fn fail_bounds(i64 index, u64 len, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) noreturn {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "runtime error");
    message_str("index ");
    message_i64(index);
    message_str(" out of range for length ");
    message_u64(len);
    fail_end();
}

fn fail_span(i64 lo, i64 hi, u64 len, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) noreturn {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "runtime error");
    message_str("span bounds ");
    message_i64(lo);
    message_str("..");
    message_i64(hi);
    message_str(" out of range for length ");
    message_u64(len);
    fail_end();
}

fn fail_overflow(char* file, u32 line, u32 col) noreturn {
    fail_runtime(file, line, col, "integer overflow");
}

fn fail_shift(i64 count, char* type_name, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) noreturn {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "runtime error");
    message_str("shift count ");
    message_i64(count);
    message_str(" out of range for ");
    message_cstr(type_name);
    fail_end();
}

fn fail_div_zero(char* file, u32 line, u32 col) noreturn {
    fail_runtime(file, line, col, "division by zero");
}

fn fail_div_overflow(char* file, u32 line, u32 col) noreturn {
    fail_runtime(file, line, col, "division overflow");
}

fn fail_alloc_count(i64 n, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) noreturn {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "runtime error");
    message_str("negative allocation count ");
    message_i64(n);
    fail_end();
}

/// Reports an assignment to an own reference-typed lvalue that is not zero.
/// The compiler emits this call in checked builds only.
/// D17.11
fn fail_overwrite(char* file, u32 line, u32 col) noreturn {
    fail_runtime(file, line, col, "overwriting owned value");
}

/// Reports a value that no clause of a switch over an enum names. Such a value
/// is reachable: a zeroed enum holds 0 whether or not 0 is a member, and a
/// cast from an integer to an enum is unchecked. The case is not undefined
/// behavior, so the runtime reports it and names both the enum and the value.
/// D7.7, D3.9, D10.7
fn fail_enum(i64 v, char* type_name, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) noreturn {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "runtime error");
    message_str("enum value ");
    message_i64(v);
    message_str(" is not a member of ");
    message_cstr(type_name);
    fail_end();
}

/// Reports the panic builtin. The message is a fort string, so it arrives as a
/// pointer and a length and may hold any byte, a NUL included. This module may
/// take the name panic because it implements that builtin and never calls it
/// (toolchain.md 5.1).
/// D12.2
fn panic(char* ptr, u64 len, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) noreturn {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "panic");
    if (len > 0) {
        buffer_append(&message, cast(ptr[0..len], u8@));
    }
    fail_end();
}

/// Reports a failed assert. text is the source text of the assert argument,
/// byte for byte.
/// D11.4
fn assert_fail(char* text, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) noreturn {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "assertion failed");
    message_cstr(text);
    fail_end();
}

// ---- allocation -------------------------------------------------------------
// D10.2, D10.3

/// Answers with zeroed storage for count elements of elem_size bytes. It asks
/// for one byte when the product is zero, so the result is never null and
/// new(T, 0) is never null. A product that overflows and a failed allocation
/// are both runtime errors at the position the caller passed
/// (toolchain.md 5.1, 5.2). The name is alloc and not new, because new is a
/// keyword.
///
/// The result is void mut* own: the caller owns the storage and may write it,
/// which is what an allocation is for. The mark comes from libc.calloc and is
/// carried out rather than dropped, so a caller casts to a typed pointer to
/// write through it and that cast adds no mut. A caller that only reads drops
/// the mark on the way.
/// D2.4, D3.11, D5.4
fn alloc(u64 elem_size, u64 count, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) void mut* own {
    if (elem_size != 0 && count > U64_MAX / elem_size) {
        fail_runtime(file, line, col, "allocation size overflow");
    }
    u64 mut size = elem_size * count;
    if (size == 0) {
        size = 1;
    }
    void mut* own p = libc.calloc(1, size);
    if (p == null) {
        fail_runtime(file, line, col, "out of memory");
    }
    return p;
}

/// Releases storage that alloc answered with. A null p does nothing, as C's
/// free does nothing. The name is free and not del, because a module-level
/// declaration named del would shadow the universe function, and this module
/// calls del on its own storage.
/// D12.2, D7.9
fn free(void* own p) void {
    libc.free(move(p));
}

// ---- strings ----------------------------------------------------------------
// D3.7

/// Answers true when the two strings have the same length and the same bytes.
/// == and != on strings compare exactly that, so the zero string equals "".
/// This function compares the bytes with libc.memcmp. It must not use ==,
/// because == calls this function.
/// D3.7
fn str_eq(char* a, u64 a_len, char* b, u64 b_len) bool {
    if (a_len != b_len) {
        return false;
    }
    if (a_len == 0) {
        // Two empty strings are equal whatever their pointers are, and memcmp
        // may not be given a null pointer even for zero bytes.
        return true;
    }
    return libc.memcmp(cast(a, u8*), cast(b, u8*), a_len) == 0;
}

// ---- process ----------------------------------------------------------------
// D11.6, D8.6

/// Builds the argument span from argv. The main the compiler emits calls this
/// once, before fort_entry. Each element views the argv bytes themselves,
/// which are NUL-terminated, so a raw pointer reaches a span through the
/// two-bound form and a string through strlen.
/// D11.6, D6.9
///
/// This releases what an earlier call allocated. The C runtime overwrote and
/// leaked instead. The release matters for more than the leak: without it the
/// assignment below carries the overwrite check, which the compiler emits in
/// checked builds only, so the two build modes would differ.
/// D17.11
///
/// The release costs one thing. A span that an earlier args() handed out
/// points into the storage this del frees, so a second args_init invalidates
/// it. The C runtime leaked instead, and its old span stayed readable. The
/// main the compiler emits calls args_init once, so no program the compiler
/// builds can reach that case; a program that calls args_init itself can.
/// D11.6
///
/// The allocation is an ordinary new, so a failed allocation here names the
/// line of std/rt.ft that made it. No decision states that position.
fn args_init(i32 argc, char* mut* argv) void {
    del(args_store);
    if (argc <= 0) {
        return;
    }
    u64 n = cast(argc, u64);
    string mut@ own a = new(string, n);
    char* mut@ view = argv[0..n];
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        char* p = view[i];
        a[i] = cast(p[0..libc.strlen(p)], string);
    }
    args_store = move(a);
}

/// Answers with the span main received. sys.args() hands it on. The result is
/// a view. The runtime keeps the storage (stdlib.md 3).
/// D8.6, D11.6, D17.3
fn args() string@ {
    return args_store;
}

/// Flushes every buffer and ends the process with status & 0xFF. sys.exit
/// calls it.
/// D11.6
fn exit(i32 status) noreturn {
    flush_all();
    libc.exit(status & EXIT_STATUS_MASK);
}
